/-!
# The Lovász–Szegedy limit theorem

Every convergent sequence of nonempty simple graphs has a graphon limit
(`exists_graphon_limit`): if `t(F, Gₙ)` converges for every simple graph `F`, there is a graphon
`W` (in the sense of `IsGraphon`: jointly measurable, symmetric, values in `[0, 1]`) with
`t(F, Gₙ) → t(F, W)` for every `F` (L. Lovász and B. Szegedy, *Limits of dense graph sequences*,
J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 96 (2006), 933-957, arXiv:math/0408173; C. Borgs, J. T. Chayes,
L. Lovász, V. T. Sós and K. Vesztergombi, *Convergent sequences of dense graphs I*, Adv. Math. 219
(2008), 1801-1851, arXiv:math/0702004).

The proof follows Lovász and Szegedy (arXiv:math/0408173v2, §5.4: Lemma 5.1, Lemma 5.2 and
Claim 5.3): weakly regular partitions of the graphs that refine each other, a subsequence along
which their density matrices converge, and the almost everywhere limit of a martingale of step
functions. Lovász and Szegedy use equitable partitions (their Lemma 4.2), so that their steps are
intervals of equal length; here the partitions come from the Frieze–Kannan lemma, whose classes
may have any sizes, so the cells of `[0, 1]` have the limit masses as lengths.

* For each graph, the energy increment argument of the Frieze–Kannan weak regularity lemma
  (`MathlibExt.Combinatorics.SimpleGraph.GraphLimits.WeakRegularity`), restarted from the previous
  partition, gives nested partitions encoded by bit labels of the vertices: the partition by the
  first `bitLevel (k + 1)` bits is weakly `1 / (k + 1)`-regular.
* Along a subsequence, the mass of every cell (the vertices with given first bits) and the edge
  mass between any two cells of the same level converge.
* The limit masses define nested cells of `[0, 1]` by binary search, and the limit averaged
  weights define step kernels on them. The step kernels form a martingale for the filtration
  generated by the cells, so they converge almost everywhere (the martingale convergence theorem
  of Mathlib); their clamped upper limit is the graphon `W`.
* By the counting lemma, `t(F, Gₙ)` is within `|E(F)| / (k + 1)` of the density of `F` in the
  partition average of `Gₙ`, a polynomial in the masses and averaged weights of the cells. In the
  limit this polynomial is the density of `F` in the step kernel, which tends to `t(F, W)` by the
  counting lemma for graphons, since the martingale also converges in `L¹`.
-/

public section

namespace MathlibExt.Combinatorics.SimpleGraph.GraphLimitsWanted

